  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "📰 Newspaper Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Names for a local paper, a school or class newspaper, a fantasy world, an old-fashioned paper or a newsletter."
    },
    "intro": "Many newspaper names still follow a pattern that's centuries old: a place, then a word for news, like Gazette, Courier or Chronicle. This **newspaper name generator** uses those words and plenty of newer ones for a local paper, a school or class newspaper, a paper in a fantasy world, an old-fashioned title, a funny one or a newsletter.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Eight good newspaper names",
        "content": "<p>All eight came from the generator, one from each kind of paper plus a class name:</p><ul><li><strong>Hampton Gazette</strong>: your town and the oldest word for a newspaper. Type your own town into the box.</li><li><strong>The Oakfield Scoop</strong>: a school paper that sounds as if it knows something you don't.</li><li><strong>The Giggle Gazette</strong>: a kids' paper with the alliteration children love.</li><li><strong>Hedgehogs Class Herald</strong>: for a primary class named after an animal or a tree.</li><li><strong>The Highmere Crier</strong>: a fantasy town's paper, named after the town crier it replaced.</li><li><strong>The Ashbury Mercury</strong>: an old-fashioned title using the messenger of the Roman gods.</li><li><strong>The Weekly Whinge</strong>: a funny paper that admits what it's really for.</li><li><strong>In the Loop</strong>: a friendly newsletter name for an office, club or street.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "What the old newspaper words mean",
